## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model constraints with DP, including exact count, non-adjacency, and reconstruction; this is a common interview pattern for medium-difficulty tasks.

## Key Concepts

- 2D DP with adjacency constraint
- Exact-count optimization
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPromo(revenue, k, cost){
  const n = revenue.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      // take i
      if (t > 0){
        const takeFrom = i >= 2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t-1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG);
        const val = takeFrom + revenue[i-1] - cost;
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], val);
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i = n, t = k;
  const picks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t >= 0){
    if (dp[i][t] === dp[i-1][t]){ i--; }
    else { picks.unshift(i); i -= 2; t--; }
  }
  return { best: dp[n][k], picks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize space to O(k)?
- How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests interval DP with exactly k block selection, reconstruction, and non-adjacent constraint handling. Requires precomputing interval values (max-min) and a clean end-at-i transition that accounts for the gap requirement.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP with exactly k non-adjacent blocks
- Precomputation of val(l,r) = max(A[l..r]) - min(A[l..r]) using O(n²) sliding window enumeration
- Reconstruction pointers to retrieve endpoints
- Time complexity: O(n²) precomputation + O(kn²) DP; Space: O(n²) for val table + O(n·k) for DP
- Edge cases: k > ⌊n/2⌋ (impossible, return error), empty arrays (return 0)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSumIntervals(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const val = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let min = A[l], max = A[l];
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      min = Math.min(min, A[r]);
      max = Math.max(max, A[r]);
      val[l][r] = max - min;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      parent[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const prev = j >= 2 ? dp[j-2][t-1] : (t === 1 ? 0 : -Infinity);
        if (prev === -Infinity) continue;
        const curr = prev + val[j-1][i-1];
        if (curr > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = curr;
          parent[i][t] = { start: j, end: i, prev: j - 2 };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const intervals = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && parent[i][t]) {
    const p = parent[i][t];
    if (!p.skip) {
      intervals.push([p.start, p.end]);
      i = p.prev;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], intervals: intervals.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on ordered subsequences with reconstruction, a common interview pattern that scales gracefully from beginner to moderate.

## Key Concepts
- State dp[i][t] represents best sum ending at i as the t-th pick
- Transition uses max over previous indices; reconstruction via parent pointers
- Space/time trade-offs; potential O(nk) optimization via running maxima

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDotSubsequence(A,B,k){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ dp[i][1] = A[i]*B[i]; par[i][1] = -1; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
      let best=-Infinity; let arg=-1;
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        if(dp[j][t-1] > best){ best=dp[j][t-1]; arg=j; }
      }
      if(best>-Infinity){
        dp[i][t] = best + A[i]*B[i];
        par[i][t] = arg;
      }
    }
  }
  let bestEnd=-1, bestVal=-Infinity;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(dp[i][k] > bestVal){ bestVal=dp[i][k]; bestEnd=i; }
  const indices=[]; let i=bestEnd, t=k;
  while(i>=0 && t>=1){ indices.push(i); i=par[i][t]; t--; }
  indices.reverse();
  return {value: bestVal, indices};
}
```

